    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nGM Is Shutting Down Its Battery Plant in Detroit\n10 photos\nPhoto: General Motors\nGeneral Motors is shutting down its Brownstown Battery Assembly plant in Detroit, Michigan. The auto giant is planning to wind down operations at the 480,000-square-foot facility by next summer, when its current lease expires.\nThe Brownstown facility, which opened back in 2009, was primarily dedicated to low-volume, niche electrified components. The facility assembled high-voltage battery packs for GM’s ultra-luxury flagship EV, the Cadillac Celestiq. It also produced both the electric motors and battery packs for the first-ever hybrid Corvette and housed a short-lived joint venture with Honda to develop hydrogen fuel cell technology.\nThe shutdown stems from shifting vehicle production lifecycles, dissolving partnerships, and EV market meltdown. In production for nearly three years at the GM Bowling Green Assembly Plant in Kentucky, the Corvette E-Ray has been phased out after a short-lived glory and replaced by the Corvette Grand Sport X.\nIn the meantime, the Celestiq , which sees the light of day at the auto group’s tech center in Warren, Michigan, remains an exclusive, low-run flagship product.. Only 25 units of the Cadillac Celestiq were produced for its initial 2025 model year, with total lifetime deliveries across both the 2025 and 2026 model years estimated to still be well under 100 units.\nFurthermore, the hydrogen partnership with Honda is dissolving, bringing nearly a decade of formal collaboration to a close. Operating under the name Fuel Cell System Manufacturing LLC (FCSM) and taking up roughly 80,000 square feet of the Brownstown plant, the two automakers agreed to completely discontinue the fuel cell system project at their Michigan facility.\nDue to progressive production cuts, staffing at the plant had already been reduced from a peak of 300 down to fewer than a dozen employees. Under General Motors' agreement with the UAW union, these remaining workers are being offered transfers to other nearby facilities. They were notified about the change earlier this year.\nThe lease expiration officially brings operations at one of GM's earliest electrification sites to a close as GM is adjusting its original aggressive EV strategy in response to softer-than-expected consumer demand and changing federal regulations, which prompted a shift back to gas-powered trucks and high-margin models.\nThe ongoing assembly and component production handled by the Brownstown site will be transferred to other active General Motors facilities across the country, Automotive News reports. In the meantime, the auto giant has incurred a total of $11.2 billion in EV-related charges for scaling back its electric vehicle footprint and addressing supplier settlements.\nA person familiar with the matter confirmed for Automotive News that the plant will be decommissioned by next summer, when the lease comes to an end. The decision comes after GM announced it would pull the plug on the $2.5 billion battery plant in Lansing, now exclusively operated by LG Energy Solution.\nAs a backup plan, General Motors took over the long-term lease of the building, originally planned to be used for its subsequently canceled Piston Automotive partnership, but has yet to decide what it will do with it.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nGeneral Motors General Motors plant Chevrolet Corvette E-Ray Cadillac Celestiq GM\nAbout the author: Elena Luchian\nElena started off as a sports reporter and five years later switched to automotive. How does that happen? Lots of writing, testdrives, traveling, off-road driving courses, track driving courses, defensive driving courses and diplomas. The diplomas might be \"useless\", but the knowledge she gained is priceless. Full profile\nUP NEXT New BMW M3 Gearing Up To Rival AMG's C 63 and Audi's RS 5 – Rendering",
